Z||000000||Luperman||03-31-2000||12:10 AM||||had to have my car towed the other day when it left me stranded (sniff sniff) had to leave it on the side of the road , but any way the dealership I went to replaced the ignition module and pickup coil and I also told them to test drive it because I,ve been having a surging problem with it when you drive it at no load speed gas pedal steady at 45 - 70 mph,  the mechanic told me he tested the computer with a scan tool and found no problems then drove it and said I had a problem  with the transmission that was causing it to surge. okay I picked the car up and it ran a hell of alot better and when it started to surge I pushed lightly on the brake pedal enough to get the converter unlocked and the surge seemed to go away no my question would be do you think there is a problem with my torque converter clutch locked and unlocking on it's own causing my surging problems or maybe that I have'nt changed the tranny fiuld and filter in 50,000miles.  sorry about the book but replies would be appreciated.||||reg||5
Z||000001||WKDFIRO||03-31-2000||01:00 PM||||Automatic transmissions can be pretty mysterious. The only thing I could even guess would be a sticking solenoid on the convertor lockup. The usual symptom that we see at the shop is that the Lockup solenoid will stick and the car will stall at a stop. Its one of the few things that you can get to without major surgery on the tranny. Check you manual for details on how to get to it. If that is the problem than you will have saved yourself the trouble of having the tranny taken out. If it isn't....Good luck!||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||03-31-2000||10:07 PM||||surge is not caused by the TC lock however tc lock will make surge more obvious. When the TC is unlocked the surge is mostly lost in the TC. When the TC is locked every change in RPM goes directly to the wheels.||||reg||1
